~ NIR scene-imaging headset. Pulsed 850 nm LEDs light a target 1 to 10 m away, and a 5 MP sensor behind a 16 mm lens collects the reflected light. The compute and cellular parts come from the pole-mounted camera teardown [@CEHRP-Flock].

reference constants as k

submodel led
submodel M12B1618 as lens
submodel sensor as s
submodel display as d
submodel battery as b
submodel open_q_624a as som
submodel s7648 as lte

section Requirements

Working distance: w = 1 | 10 :m

    ~ Requirement. Nearest and farthest target.

Scene reflectance (0, 1): rho = 0.1 | 0.6

    ~ Assumed range from dark clothing to bright surfaces at the LED wavelength. Replace with a measured or cited range.

Ambient temperature: T_a = T_0.k + (15:K) | T_0.k + (30:K) :K

    ~ Assumed indoor use, 15 °C to 30 °C.

Required signal-to-noise ratio: S_r = 10

    ~ Assumed minimum for recognizable imagery.

Required runtime: t_r = 4 :hr

    ~ Requirement. One work shift.

Mass limit: m_max = 250 :g

    ~ Requirement. Comfort when worn.

Eye irradiance limit: E_max = 100 :W/m^2

    ~ Corneal and lens limit for 780 nm to 3000 nm and exposures longer than 1000 s, page 4 of [@Osram-AN090], who cite IEC 62471. The retinal thermal limit on source radiance is not modeled.

section Design choices

LED count: n = 4

    ~ Design choice, set by the signal-to-noise ratio needed.

Exposure time: t_e = 10 :ms

    ~ Design choice, short enough to limit motion blur. The LEDs are strobed during it.

Frame rate: f_r = 30 :Hz

    ~ Design choice.

section Illumination

Duty cycle (0, 1): D = t_e * f_r / (2*pi)

    ~ Fraction of time the LEDs are on. Oneil stores Hz as rad/s, so dividing by $2\pi$ returns cycles.

Photon energy: E_p = h.k * c.k / lam.led :J

Spot radius: r_s = w * tan(theta.led) :m

    ~ Circular spot at the working distance.

In-pulse scene irradiance: E_s = n * Phi.led / (pi * r_s^2) :W/m^2

    ~ Uniform spot assumed. The real center is brighter, so this underestimates the peak.

$ Time-averaged eye irradiance: E_a = D * E_s :W/m^2

    ~ Pulsed sources are compared with the continuous limit using the duty-cycle average, provided one pulse is within the limit alone, page 9 of [@Osram-AN090].

section Imaging

Sensor irradiance: E_i = rho * E_s * tau.lens / (4 * N.lens^2) :W/m^2

    ~ Lambertian target on the optical axis, thin lens, target much farther than the focal length.

Photons per pixel: N_p = E_i * p.s^2 * t_e * eta.s / E_p

$ Signal-to-noise ratio: S = sqrt(N_p)

    ~ Shot-noise limited. Read noise and ambient light are ignored, so this is optimistic.

Half field of view: theta_f = atan(d.s / (2 * f.lens))

    ~ Along the sensor diagonal.

section Power and mass

LED electrical power: P_l = n * D * Phi.led / eta.led :W

    ~ Average draw, not peak.

Total power: P_t = P_l + P.s + P.d + P.som + P.lte :W

$ Total mass: m_t = n * m.led + m.lens + m.s + m.d + m.b + m.som + m.lte :g

$ Runtime: t = E.b * eta.b / P_t :hr

    ~ Usable battery energy over the average total draw.

section Tests

test: t >= t_r

test: S >= S_r

test: m_t <= m_max

test: E_a <= E_max

test: theta_f <= theta.led

    ~ The LED beam must cover the whole field of view.

test: w >= d_m.lens

    ~ Targets must be beyond the lens minimum object distance.

test: d.s <= d_c.lens

    ~ The sensor must fit inside the lens image circle.

test: n_x.s * n_y.s <= M.som

    ~ The sensor resolution must be within the compute module camera limit.

test: V.b >= min(V.som) and V.b <= max(V.som)

    ~ The nominal cell voltage must be inside the compute module supply range.

test: min(T_a) >= min(T.som) and max(T_a) <= max(T.som)

    ~ The ambient range must fit inside the compute module operating range.

test: min(T_a) >= min(T.lte) and max(T_a) <= max(T.lte)

    ~ The ambient range must fit inside the modem operating range.
